parabola that peaks  at 1 1 and passes crosses the x-axis at  x equals 0 and x equals 2 if I now want  to compute this volume as I rotate the  region R about the y axis  so I'm going to I'm going to rotate this  about the y axis I want to find out what  volume is generated by this region R in  this case remember I can use either  cylindrical shells or I can use  revolution here notice the problem I'm  going to be in that notice that this  particular function is single valued but  not one-to-one when I try to find these  two x-values I'm going to run into multi  values I'm going to have to invert all  sorts of computational skills are going  to come into play here on the other hand  if I take my generating element parallel  to the y axis I have a very simple  expression for this and now that  indicates what cylindrical shells by the  method of cylindrical shells this will  be what the Vaught the integral from 0  to 2 the generating arm is X so that  cuts out 2 pi X the height is Y which is  2x minus x squared the thickness is DX  in other words mechanically I must  evaluate this particular integral ok at  any rate it's factoring out the 2 pi and  just observing that the integral of 2x  squared is 2/3 X cubed the integral of x  to the fourth is 1/4 X to the fourth  evaluating that between 0 &amp; 2  I get what 2 pi times 16 thirds minus 4  the lower limit is 0 here this just  comes out to be 16 minus 12 4/3 times 2  pi that's 8 PI over 3 by the way before  I go any further with this let's make  the interesting observation see 8 PI  over 3 is the volumes  by this whole thing being revolved about  the y-axis if I had run in this line  which is a line of symmetry notice that  these two areas are congruent these two  regions are congruent however it's also  interesting to observe that the volume  generated by this piece as you revolve  it about the y-axis is not twice the  volume generated by this piece see  notice that the integral from 0 to 1  other words if we just took this region  here  integrating this from 0 to 1 we would  get 5 6 2/3 minus 1/4 times 2 pi 5/6 and  if we double that we would get 5/3 in  other words this area is 5/6 double it  would be 5 5 PI over 6 double it would  be 5 PI over 3 and 5 PI over 3 is not  the same as 8 PI over 3 the thing to  keep in mind here is notice how the  distance comes in again you see for  example these two lines here these two  lines here are symmetric with respect to  the line x equals 1 but notice that this  generates a much larger volume than this  because it's generating arm is longer  it's further away but any rate that's  just on the side notice how by the  method of cylindrical shells we  determine that the volume is 8 PI over 3  suppose we'd want to do this by the  solid method the solid of revolution  method notice that we would first have  to invert this relationship we would  first have to solve for X in terms of Y  notice that y equals 2x - x squared is  the same as saying that x squared minus  2x plus y is 0 using the quadratic  formula we can solve for x and we now  find that X is 1 plus the square root of  1 minus y or 1 minus the square root of  1 minus y what that means by the way  geometrically is simply this for a given  value of y there are two values of X  located symmetrically with respect to x  equals 1  see there are symmetric portions well  this doesn't make that much difference  the thing now is what what is my  cross-sectional area my cross-sectional  area is pi times this length squared  minus pi times this length squared  that's pi times 1 plus the square root  of 1 minus y squared minus pi times the  square root of 1 minus the square root  of 1 minus y squared when I square this  and subtract all but the middle term  drops out in other words I have twice  the square root of 1 minus y here minus  twice the square root of 1 minus y here  when I subtract I get 4 times the square  root of 1 minus y I multiply that by PI  that's my cross sectional area and now  to find the volume I just integrate that  as Y goes from 0 to 1 you see and if I  carry out this integration noticing that  the integral of 1 minus y to the one  half is minus 2/3 remember the  derivative of 1 minus y with respect to  Y is minus 1 to the 3 1 minus y to the 3  halves  evaluate that between 0 and 1 the upper  limit gives me 0 the lower limit is  minus 2/3 I subtract the lower limit  gives me 2/3 4pi times 2/3 is 8 PI over  3 the same answer as I got before notice  by the way that this was messy but we  could handle it if this had been much  tougher say a 6 over here or something  like that instead of a tool how would we  have solve for X in terms of Y you see  in other words this would have been a  case where the shell method would have  been necessitated because of the  impossibility of the algebra but at any  rate we have plenty of opportunity to  illustrate that in terms of exercises  and supplementary notes and reading  material and what have you that is  actually the easiest part the hard part  is to understand the significance of  what's going on so I thought that to  summarize today's lecture let's keep in  mind that whether you call an area or  whether you call it volume or whether  you call it distance traveled and  velocity the fact remains that if F is a  function  continuous on the closed interval from A  to B and we partition that interval into  n parts and we form the sum as K goes  from 1 to N F of C sub K times Delta X  sub K where C sub K is in the case  interval and Delta X sub K is just X sub  K minus X sub K minus 1 if we take that  limit as the largest Delta X approaches  0 and call that Q that limit Q exists  symbolically it's written by the  definite integral from A to B f of X DX  and more to the point if you happen to  know differential calculus you can  compute Q just by computing G of B minus  G of a where G prime is any function  whose derivative is f okay now again  this is what I'm calling the summary if  you separate this out from all of the  computational stuff that we did in  today's lecture this is the part that's  left ok and what I want to do next time  is to show you that things are not quite  this straightforward all the time that  certain nice things have been happening  here that allow us essentially to get  away with murder and what I mean by that  will become clearer next time but until  next time then goodbye funding for the  publication of this video was provided  by the Gabriella and Paul Rosenbaum  foundation  help ocw continue to provide free and  open access to MIT courses by making a  donation at ocw.mit.edu/donate
 

